      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A ferry reached City Island in 1763, making it the first New York island with regular mainland service.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityislandchamber.org/about-city-island/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The island’s original name, Minnewits, changed to City Island after Benjamin Palmer’s failed seaport plan, reflecting its early ambition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityislandchamber.org/about-city-island/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Native Americans of the Lenape left shell middens on City Island, proving the area was a historic oyster and clam gathering spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Island%2C_Bronx",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Public School 17, built in 1897, now houses the City Island Historical Society and Nautical Museum, preserving the island’s maritime heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
